<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced">
  <title>A second example</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The finite-jet construction above loses uniformity because separated completion creates a
nilpotent.  We now give a second construction in which the bad rational localisation is an
integral domain.  The loss of uniformity is instead caused by an unbounded family of
nonnilpotent power-bounded elements.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-rationally-reduced-example">
      <statement>
        <p>
          There is a complete Tate <m>k</m>-algebra <m>\mathcal A</m> with the following properties.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathcal A</m> is a uniform, nonnoetherian integral domain and
      <m>\mathcal A^\circ=\mathcal A_0</m> for the ring of definition displayed below.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The Huber pair <m>(\mathcal A,\mathcal A^\circ)</m> is sheafy.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Every finite iterated rational localisation of <m>\mathcal A</m> is reduced.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The rational localisation
      <me>\mathcal B=\mathcal A\left\langle\frac{W}{\varpi}\right\rangle</me>
      is an integral domain but is not uniform.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          In particular, failure of stable uniformity need not be caused by a nilpotent in the bad
rational localisation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced-the-weighted-parity-algebra">
    <title>The weighted-parity algebra</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>I=\mathbf N_{>0}</m>.  For a finite-support multi-index
<m>\nu=(\nu_n)_{n\in I}\in\mathbf N^{(I)}</m>, put
      <men xml:id="eq-parity-weight">\omega(\nu)=\sum_{\substack{n\geq1\\ \nu_n\text{ odd}}} n.</men>
      Consider the submonoid
      <men xml:id="eq-parity-monoid">S=\left\{(a,\nu)\in\mathbf N\times\mathbf N^{(I)}:
       a\geq\omega(\nu)\right\}.</men>
      The inequality
      <men xml:id="eq-omega-subadditive">\omega(\nu+\mu)\leq\omega(\nu)+\omega(\mu)</men>
      shows that <m>S</m> is closed under addition.  It is locally finite: a fixed element of <m>S</m> has
only finitely many decompositions as a sum of two elements of <m>S</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>k[S]</m> be the monoid algebra with basis <m>W^aU^\nu</m>, where
<m>U^\nu=\prod_nU_n^{\nu_n}</m>.  Give it the Gauss norm
      <men xml:id="eq-parity-gauss-norm">\left\lVert\sum_{(a,\nu)\in S}c_{a,\nu}W^aU^\nu\right\rVert_G
  =\sup_{a,\nu}|c_{a,\nu}|.</men>
      Its completion is
      <men xml:id="eq-parity-algebra">\mathcal A=
  \left\{
    \sum_{(a,\nu)\in S}c_{a,\nu}W^aU^\nu:
    c_{a,\nu}\in k,\quad c_{a,\nu}\longrightarrow0
  \right\},</men>
      where convergence to zero is over the discrete index set <m>S</m>.  Local finiteness makes the
coefficientwise convolution finite, and the submultiplicativity of the Gauss norm on <m>k[S]</m>
extends multiplication continuously to <m>\mathcal A</m>.  Put
      <men xml:id="eq-parity-ring-of-definition">\mathcal A_0=
  \left\{
    \sum_{(a,\nu)\in S}c_{a,\nu}W^aU^\nu\in\mathcal A:
    c_{a,\nu}\in k^\circ
  \right\}.</men>
      Then <m>\mathcal A_0</m> is the closed unit ball for <xref ref="eq-parity-gauss-norm"/>, it is
<m>\varpi</m>-adically complete, and
<m>\mathcal A=\mathcal A_0[1/\varpi]</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The finite stages admit a useful affinoid presentation.  For <m>N\geq1</m>, put
      <men xml:id="eq-finite-head-presentation">\mathcal A_N=
  \frac{k\langle W,Y_1,Z_1,\ldots,Y_N,Z_N\rangle}
       {(Y_n^2-W^{2n}Z_n)_{1\leq n\leq N}}.</men>
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-finite-stage-normal-form">
      <title>Isometric finite-stage normal form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          There is an isometric decomposition
          <men xml:id="eq-finite-stage-normal-form">\mathcal A_N\cong
  \bigoplus_{\epsilon\in\{0,1\}^N}^{\max}
  k\langle W,Z_1,\ldots,Z_N\rangle Y^\epsilon.</men>
          Under the substitution
          <men xml:id="eq-finite-stage-substitution">Y_n\longmapsto W^nU_n,
  \qquad
  Z_n\longmapsto U_n^2,</men>
          this identifies <m>\mathcal A_N</m> isometrically with the closed subalgebra of
<m>k\langle W,U_1,\ldots,U_N\rangle</m> supported on the monomials in
<xref ref="eq-parity-monoid"/> involving only <m>U_1,\ldots,U_N</m>.  The transition maps
<m>\mathcal A_N\to\mathcal A_{N+1}</m> are isometric, and
          <men xml:id="eq-A-completion-of-heads">\mathcal A=\widehat{\bigcup_{N\geq1}\mathcal A_N}.</men>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Writing <m>\nu_n=2q_n+\epsilon_n</m> gives the unique factorization
          <men xml:id="eq-parity-factorization">W^aU^\nu
  =W^{a-\omega(\nu)}
   \prod_{n=1}^NY_n^{\epsilon_n}Z_n^{q_n}</men>
          whenever <m>a\geq\omega(\nu)</m> and <m>\nu</m> is supported in <m>\{1,\ldots,N\}</m>.  Thus the
finite-head support algebra is the sum of the subspaces
<me>k\langle W,Z_1,\ldots,Z_N\rangle Y^\epsilon,
  \qquad \epsilon\in\{0,1\}^N.</me>
The summands have disjoint monomial supports, distinguished by the parities
of the <m>U_n</m>-exponents.  The sum is therefore direct and its Gauss norm is
the maximum of the norms of its components.
        </p>
        <p>
          The substitution <xref ref="eq-finite-stage-substitution"/> kills
<m>Y_n^2-W^{2n}Z_n</m> and hence gives a norm-nonincreasing map from the quotient
<xref ref="eq-finite-head-presentation"/> to this support algebra.  In the other
direction, send a parity decomposition
<m>\sum_\epsilon f_\epsilon Y^\epsilon</m> to the class represented by the same
sum.  This map is also norm-nonincreasing, and
<xref ref="eq-parity-factorization"/> shows that the two maps are inverse.
This proves the isometric normal form.  The same support description makes
the transition maps isometric.  Finally, every finite set of allowed
monomials lies in one head, and finite-support series are dense in
<xref ref="eq-parity-algebra"/>; hence
<xref ref="eq-A-completion-of-heads"/> follows.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-parity-uniform-domain">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The ring <m>\mathcal A</m> is a complete uniform Tate <m>k</m>-algebra and an integral domain, with
<me>\mathcal A^\circ=\mathcal A_0.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.isTateRing_WPA">Lean (Tate structure)</lean>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_isUniform_of_dvr">Lean (uniformity)</lean>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_isDomain">Lean (domain)</lean>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_powerBounded_eq_unitBall">Lean (power-bounded elements)</lean>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The Gauss norm on the countable restricted Tate algebra
<m>k\langle W,U_1,U_2,\ldots\rangle</m> is multiplicative.  To see this, note
first that the norm of a nonzero restricted series is attained.  For each of
two such series choose, among the norm-attaining exponents, the
lexicographically largest one.  In the coefficient of the sum of these two
exponents, the product of the chosen coefficients strictly dominates every
other convolution term.  The ultrametric inequality therefore gives
<me>\lVert fg\rVert_G=\lVert f\rVert_G\lVert g\rVert_G.</me>
This is the countable restricted-series argument used in the formalisation,
whose underlying Lean implementation is adapted from work of William Coram
<xref ref="bib-CoramXia"/>.  The support algebra <m>\mathcal A</m> is a closed isometric
subring of this Tate algebra, so its Gauss norm is multiplicative and it is a
domain.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>x\in\mathcal A_0</m>, all powers of <m>x</m> remain in <m>\mathcal A_0</m>.  If
<m>x\notin\mathcal A_0</m>, multiplicativity gives
<m>\lVert x^m\rVert_G=\lVert x\rVert_G^m</m>, which is unbounded.  Hence
<m>\mathcal A^\circ=\mathcal A_0</m>.  Completeness and the Tate property follow from
<xref ref="eq-parity-algebra"/> and the topologically nilpotent unit <m>\varpi</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-parity-nonnoetherian">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The ring <m>\mathcal A</m> is not noetherian.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_not_noetherian">Lean</lean>
      <proof>
        <p>
          For <m>m\geq1</m>, let
<me>I_m=(Z_1,\ldots,Z_m)\subset\mathcal A.</me>
Define a norm-nonincreasing homomorphism
<me>\psi_m:\mathcal A\longrightarrow k\langle T\rangle,</me>
by retaining only the coefficients of the monomials
<m>U_{m+1}^{2q}</m> and sending that monomial to <m>T^q</m>.  The support condition
shows directly that this is multiplicative: a product can contribute such a
monomial only when both factors contribute monomials of the same form.
Thus <m>\psi_m</m> sends <m>W</m> and every <m>Y_j</m> to zero, sends <m>Z_{m+1}</m> to <m>T</m>,
and sends every other <m>Z_j</m> to zero.  It kills <m>I_m</m> but not <m>Z_{m+1}</m>.
Therefore
<me>I_1\subsetneq I_2\subsetneq I_3\subsetneq\cdots,</me>
so <m>\mathcal A</m> is not noetherian.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced-a-reduced-nonuniform-rational-chart">
    <title>A reduced nonuniform rational chart</title>
    <p>
      The ideal <m>(W,\varpi)</m> is open because <m>\varpi</m> is a unit of <m>\mathcal A</m>.
Define the complete <m>k^\circ</m>-algebra
      <men xml:id="eq-weighted-chart-lattice">\mathcal B_0=
  \left\{
    \sum_{\substack{\nu\in\mathbf N^{(I)},\ d\geq\omega(\nu)}}
      b_{d,\nu}X^dU^\nu:
    b_{d,\nu}\in\varpi^{\omega(\nu)}k^\circ,
    \quad
    \varpi^{-\omega(\nu)}b_{d,\nu}\longrightarrow0
  \right\},</men>
      with norm
      <men xml:id="eq-weighted-chart-norm">\left\lVert\sum b_{d,\nu}X^dU^\nu\right\rVert_{\mathrm{in}}
  =\sup_{d,\nu}|\varpi|^{-\omega(\nu)}|b_{d,\nu}|.</men>
      The subadditivity <xref ref="eq-omega-subadditive"/> shows that <m>\mathcal B_0</m> is a ring.  Put
<m>\mathcal B=\mathcal B_0[1/\varpi]</m>.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-weighted-chart-identification">
      <statement>
        <p>
          There is a canonical isometric isomorphism
          <men xml:id="eq-weighted-chart-identification">\mathcal A\left\langle\frac{W}{\varpi}\right\rangle
  \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\mathcal B,
  \qquad W\longmapsto\varpi X.</men>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The datum <m>(W;\varpi)</m> already lies in the zeroth head
<m>H_0=k\langle W\rangle</m>.  Let
<me>Q=
  H_0\langle T\rangle/
  \overline{(\varpi T-W)}</me>
be its separated completed quotient.  Evaluation at <m>W=\varpi X</m> and <m>T=X</m>
gives a continuous map <m>Q\to k\langle X\rangle</m>.  Conversely, evaluation of
<m>k\langle X\rangle</m> at the image of <m>T</m> gives a continuous map in the other
direction.  The two maps are inverse by polynomial density, and the norm
estimates in the two universal properties show that the isomorphism is
isometric.
        </p>
        <p>
          Apply the coefficientwise-localisation construction of
<xref ref="prop-coefficientwise-localization"/> to this zeroth head.  It identifies
the rational chart with the <m>c_0</m> tail algebra over <m>Q</m>.  After replacing
<m>Q</m> by <m>k\langle X\rangle</m>, its elements and norm are exactly those in
<xref ref="eq-weighted-chart-lattice"/> and
<xref ref="eq-weighted-chart-norm"/>: the tail basis element of parity <m>\nu</m>
contributes the factor <m>\varpi^{\omega(\nu)}</m>.  This gives
<xref ref="eq-weighted-chart-identification"/>, and the head evaluation sends
<m>W</m> to <m>\varpi X</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-weighted-chart-domain-nonuniform">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The ring <m>\mathcal B</m> is an integral domain but is not uniform.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_chart_isDomain">Lean (chart is a domain)</lean>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_chart_not_isUniform">Lean (chart is nonuniform)</lean>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Group a series in <m>\mathcal B</m> by its <m>U</m>-multi-index.  This gives a
coefficientwise homomorphism
<me>\mathcal B\longrightarrow
  k\langle X\rangle[[U_1,U_2,\ldots]].</me>
It is injective, and the target is a domain because
<m>k\langle X\rangle</m> is a domain.  Hence <m>\mathcal B</m> is a domain.  In the
finite-head formalisation below, this is the embedding of the corresponding
<m>c_0</m> tail algebra into formal multivariable power series; the twisting
element is <m>\varpi X</m>, which is a non-zero-divisor.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside <m>\mathcal A</m> write <m>Y_n=W^nU_n</m>, as in
<xref ref="eq-finite-stage-substitution"/>, and put
<me>T_n=\varpi^{-n}Y_n=X^nU_n\in\mathcal B.</me>
Formula <xref ref="eq-weighted-chart-norm"/> gives
<me>\lVert T_n\rVert_{\mathrm{in}}=|\varpi|^{-n},
  \qquad
  \lVert T_n^2\rVert_{\mathrm{in}}=1.</me>
It follows by separating even and odd powers that the powers of each fixed
<m>T_n</m> are bounded.  Thus every <m>T_n</m> is power-bounded.  The family
<m>(T_n)_{n\geq1}</m> is not bounded, since its norms
<m>|\varpi|^{-n}</m> are unbounded.  Hence
<m>\mathcal B^\circ</m> is unbounded and <m>\mathcal B</m> is not uniform.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced-small-perturbations-of-rational-data">
    <title>Small perturbations of rational data</title>
    <p>
      The finite-head argument rests on the following elementary stability result.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-small-perturbation">
      <title>Small perturbation lemma</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>(E,E^+)</m> be a complete Tate Huber pair and choose a closed ring of definition
<m>E_0\subseteq E^+</m>.  Let
<m>\alpha=(f_1,\ldots,f_m;g)</m> be a rational datum whose entries lie in <m>E_0</m>.  Write
<m>d_0=g</m> and <m>d_i=f_i</m> for <m>i\geq1</m>.  Suppose that for some <m>\ell\geq0</m> there are
<m>a_0,\ldots,a_m\in E_0</m> with
          <men xml:id="eq-integral-bezout">\varpi^\ell=\sum_{j=0}^m a_jd_j.</men>
          Let <m>d_j'\in E_0</m> satisfy
          <men xml:id="eq-small-perturbation-bound">d_j'-d_j\in\varpi^{\ell+1}E_0
  \qquad(0\leq j\leq m),</men>
          and write <m>\alpha'=(f_1',\ldots,f_m';g')</m>.  Then <m>\alpha'</m> is a rational datum,
<m>\alpha</m> and <m>\alpha'</m> define the same rational subset of <m>\Spa(E,E^+)</m>, and their completed
rational localisations are canonically isomorphic.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Write <m>d_j'-d_j=\varpi^{\ell+1}h_j</m> with <m>h_j\in E_0</m>.  Then
<me>\sum_j a_jd_j'
  =\varpi^\ell(1+\varpi H),
  \qquad H=\sum_j a_jh_j\in E_0.</me>
The element <m>1+\varpi H</m> is a unit of <m>E_0</m>, with inverse given by its convergent geometric
series.  Multiplying the <m>a_j</m> by this inverse gives an integral Bezout relation for the primed
datum; in particular, <m>\alpha'</m> is open.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>x</m> lie in the rational subset defined by <m>\alpha</m>.  Since <m>a_j\in E_0\subseteq E^+</m>,
relation <xref ref="eq-integral-bezout"/> gives
<me>|\varpi|^\ell\leq |g(x)|.</me>
The perturbations have value at most <m>|\varpi|^{\ell+1}&lt;|g(x)|</m>, so
<m>|g'(x)|=|g(x)|</m> and
<m>|f_i'(x)|\leq|g'(x)|</m>.  This proves one inclusion of rational subsets.  Applying the same
argument to the primed Bezout relation proves the reverse inclusion.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rational localisation is independent of the datum used to present a rational
subset.  The equality of the two subsets therefore gives restriction maps in
both directions between <m>E_\alpha</m> and <m>E_{\alpha'}</m>.  Transitivity of
rational restriction shows that their composites are the identity.  This is
the required canonical isomorphism.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced-finite-head-rational-localisation">
    <title>Finite-head rational localisation</title>
    <p>
      Fix <m>N\geq1</m>.  For a tail multi-index
<m>\mu=(\mu_n)_{n>N}\in\mathbf N^{(\{n>N\})}</m>, write
<m>\mu_n=2q_n+\epsilon_n</m> with <m>\epsilon_n\in\{0,1\}</m> and put
      <men xml:id="eq-tail-basis">e_\mu=\prod_{n>N}Y_n^{\epsilon_n}Z_n^{q_n}.</men>
      The finite-stage normal forms give an isometric Banach-module decomposition
      <men xml:id="eq-tail-decomposition">\mathcal A\cong
  \widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{\,c_0}\mathcal A_Ne_\mu.</men>
      Multiplication is determined by
      <men xml:id="eq-tail-multiplication">e_\mu e_\lambda
  =W^{\omega(\mu)+\omega(\lambda)-\omega(\mu+\lambda)}e_{\mu+\lambda}.</men>
      Here <m>\omega</m> is applied only to the tail coordinates.  Projection to the coefficient of
<m>e_0</m> is a norm-nonincreasing algebra retraction
      <men xml:id="eq-head-retraction">\rho_N:\mathcal A\longrightarrow\mathcal A_N.</men>
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-coefficientwise-localization">
      <title>Coefficientwise localisation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\alpha</m> be a rational datum in <m>\mathcal A_N</m>, and put
<m>P=(\mathcal A_N)_\alpha</m>.  There is a canonical topological algebra isomorphism
          <men xml:id="eq-coefficientwise-localization">\mathcal A_\alpha
  \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
  \widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{\,c_0}Pe_\mu.</men>
          It is natural for rational refinements represented in a finite head: if <m>P\to Q</m> is the
corresponding head restriction map, the induced restriction sends
          <men xml:id="eq-coefficientwise-restriction">\sum_\mu p_\mu e_\mu
  \longmapsto
  \sum_\mu (p_\mu|_Q)e_\mu.</men>
          The same statement holds after increasing <m>N</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let
<me>R=\mathcal A_N\langle T_1,\ldots,T_m\rangle,
  \qquad
  J=\overline{(gT_i-f_i)_i}\subset R,
  \qquad Q=R/J.</me>
Evaluation at <m>T_i=f_i/g</m> identifies <m>Q</m> with the head rational localisation
<m>P</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tail decomposition applies after adjoining the variables <m>T_i</m>.  Apply
the quotient map <m>R\to Q</m> to each tail coefficient.  Since the rational
relations lie in the head, this gives a continuous homomorphism
<me>\Phi:\mathcal A_\alpha\longrightarrow
  \widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{c_0}Qe_\mu.</me>
Conversely, the canonical map from <m>Q</m> to <m>\mathcal A_\alpha</m> may be applied
to every coefficient.  For a null family
<m>(q_\mu)_\mu</m> the resulting series
<me>\sum_\mu q_\mu e_\mu</me>
converges in <m>\mathcal A_\alpha</m>, and defines a continuous homomorphism
<m>\Psi</m> in the other direction.  The multiplication rule
<xref ref="eq-tail-multiplication"/> shows that both maps are algebra
homomorphisms.
        </p>
        <p>
          The two composites are the identity on finite tail sums with polynomial
head coefficients.  These are dense on both sides, so continuity gives
<m>\Psi\Phi=1</m> and <m>\Phi\Psi=1</m>.  Composing with <m>Q\cong P</m> proves
<xref ref="eq-coefficientwise-localization"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a rational refinement in the head, the two evaluation maps commute with
the head restriction map.  The preceding construction is therefore
coefficientwise natural and gives <xref ref="eq-coefficientwise-restriction"/>.
Presentation independence and transitivity give the same conclusion after
enlarging the head.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-finite-head-presentation">
      <title>Finite-head presentation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every rational localisation of <m>\mathcal A</m> is canonically isomorphic to a ring of the form
          <men xml:id="eq-arbitrary-finite-head-presentation">\widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{\,c_0}Pe_\mu,</men>
          where <m>P</m> is a rational localisation of some affinoid head <m>\mathcal A_N</m>.  Under this
identification the inclusions obtained by enlarging the head have norm-nonincreasing algebra
retractions, and their union is dense.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Start with a rational datum in <m>\mathcal A</m>.  Multiplying all entries by one power of
<m>\varpi</m> does not change the rational subset or localisation, so assume that the entries lie in
<m>\mathcal A_0</m>.  Choose an integral Bezout relation
<me>\varpi^\ell=\sum_j a_jd_j,
  \qquad a_j\in\mathcal A_0.</me>
By <xref ref="eq-A-completion-of-heads"/>, for some <m>N</m> the entries <m>d_j</m> admit approximations
<m>d_j'\in\mathcal A_N\cap\mathcal A_0</m> satisfying
<m>d_j'-d_j\in\varpi^{\ell+1}\mathcal A_0</m>.  By
<xref ref="lem-small-perturbation"/>, the primed datum gives the same rational subset and the same
completed localisation.  The primed integral Bezout relation produced in that lemma remains
valid after applying the retraction <m>\rho_N</m>; hence the primed datum is a rational datum already
in <m>\mathcal A_N</m>.  Now apply <xref ref="prop-coefficientwise-localization"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>M\geq N</m>, put <m>P_M=(\mathcal A_M)_\alpha</m>.  The same proposition, with head <m>M</m>,
identifies the localisation with the <m>c_0</m>-sum over the variables of index <m>>M</m>.  Coefficient
projection gives a norm-nonincreasing retraction to <m>P_M</m>, and finite tail truncation proves
that <m>\bigcup_MP_M</m> is dense.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced-sheafiness">
    <title>Sheafiness</title>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-parity-sheafy">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Huber pair <m>(\mathcal A,\mathcal A^\circ)</m> is sheafy.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="WeightedParity.weightedParity_isSheafyComplete_of_dvr">Lean</lean>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We first work with a finite rational cover <m>U=\bigcup_iU_i</m>.  Approximate the
datum for <m>U</m> and the data for all the <m>U_i</m> simultaneously in one sufficiently
large finite head.  The small perturbation lemma does not change the rational
domains, and <xref ref="prop-coefficientwise-localization"/> gives compatible
identifications
<me>\mathcal O_X(U)\cong
  \widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{c_0}Pe_\mu,
  \qquad
  \mathcal O_X(U_i)\cong
  \widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{c_0}P_ie_\mu,</me>
with coefficientwise restriction maps.  Here <m>P</m> is a rational localisation
of one affinoid head and the <m>P_i</m> are rational localisations of <m>P</m>.  The
domains defined by the <m>P_i</m> cover <m>\Spa(P,P^\circ)</m>: pull a point back along
the split coefficient retraction to the tail algebra, use the original cover,
and then apply the retraction again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>(x_i)_i</m> be a matching family and write
<m>x_i=\sum_\mu x_{i,\mu}e_\mu</m>.  Since the head <m>P</m> is affinoid and sheafy,
the matching family <m>(x_{i,\mu})_i</m> glues to a unique <m>q_\mu\in P</m> for every
<m>\mu</m>.  It remains to check that <m>(q_\mu)_\mu</m> is a null family.  Its
restrictions to every <m>P_i</m> tend to zero because each <m>x_i</m> belongs to a
<m>c_0</m>-sum.  The product restriction map
<me>P\longrightarrow\prod_iP_i</me>
is a topological embedding, so it reflects convergence to zero.  Hence
<m>q_\mu\to0</m>, and <m>\sum_\mu q_\mu e_\mu</m> is the required section over <m>U</m>.
The same coefficientwise argument proves separation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The image of the product restriction map for <m>\mathcal A</m> is therefore the
matching-family equaliser.  This equaliser is closed, and the restriction map
is continuous and injective.  The nonarchimedean closed-range theorem over
the Tate ring <m>\mathcal A</m> now makes it a topological embedding.  We have
proved the topological sheaf condition on finite rational covers.  The
general rational-basis theorem upgrades this to the all-open structure
presheaf, and its plus-ring independence gives the same conclusion for every
ring of integral elements.  In particular, <m>(\mathcal A,\mathcal A^\circ)</m>
is sheafy.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="sec-rationally-reduced-reducedness-of-all-rational-localisations">
    <title>Reducedness of all rational localisations</title>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-formal-series-reduced">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>P</m> be a reduced ring and let <m>J</m> be any index set.  Define
          <men xml:id="eq-formal-series-ring">\mathcal F_J(P)=
  \prod_{\mu\in\mathbf N^{(J)}}P\,U^\mu,</men>
          with multiplication by coefficientwise finite convolution.  Then <m>\mathcal F_J(P)</m> is reduced.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since <m>P</m> is reduced, the diagonal map
<me>P\longrightarrow
  \prod_{\mathfrak p\in\operatorname{Spec}(P)}P/\mathfrak p</me>
is injective.  Applying formal power series coefficientwise, and then
commuting formal power series with this product, gives an injection
<me>\mathcal F_J(P)\longrightarrow
  \prod_{\mathfrak p\in\operatorname{Spec}(P)}
  \mathcal F_J(P/\mathfrak p).</me>
Each factor on the right is a domain, since its coefficient ring is a
domain.  The product is therefore reduced, and so is
<m>\mathcal F_J(P)</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-parity-rationally-reduced">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every finite iterated rational localisation of <m>\mathcal A</m> is reduced.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          By transitivity of rational localisation, it is enough to treat one rational localisation.  Use
<xref ref="cor-finite-head-presentation"/> to write it as
          <men xml:id="eq-reduced-localization-decomposition">E\cong\widehat{\bigoplus}_{\mu}^{c_0}Pe_\mu,
  \qquad P=(\mathcal A_N)_\alpha.</men>
          The affinoid algebra <m>\mathcal A_N</m> is a domain by
<xref ref="lem-finite-stage-normal-form"/>.  A rational localisation of a reduced affinoid algebra is
reduced <xref ref="bib-BGR" detail="Corollary 7.3.2/10"/>; see also
<xref ref="bib-KedlayaAWS" detail="Remark 1.2.16"/>.  Thus <m>P</m> is reduced.
        </p>
        <p>
          The element <m>W</m> is a non-zero-divisor in <m>\mathcal A_N</m>.  Affinoid rational localisation is
algebraically flat <xref ref="bib-BGR" detail="Corollary 7.3.2/6"/>; see also
<xref ref="bib-ConradL15" detail="Proposition 15.1.1, proof"/>.  Tensoring
<m>0\to\mathcal A_N\xrightarrow{W}\mathcal A_N</m> with <m>P</m> therefore shows that multiplication
by <m>W</m> is injective on <m>P</m>.  If <m>P=0</m>, the conclusion is immediate, so assume otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>J=\{n>N\}</m>.  Define
          <men xml:id="eq-formal-embedding">\Phi:E\longrightarrow\mathcal F_J(P),
  \qquad
  \Phi\left(\sum_\mu x_\mu e_\mu\right)
  =\sum_\mu W^{\omega(\mu)}x_\mu U^\mu.</men>
          The multiplication rule <xref ref="eq-tail-multiplication"/> makes <m>\Phi</m> multiplicative.  If
<m>\Phi(\sum x_\mu e_\mu)=0</m>, coefficient comparison gives
<m>W^{\omega(\mu)}x_\mu=0</m> for every <m>\mu</m>.  The <m>W</m>-regularity of <m>P</m> then gives
<m>x_\mu=0</m> for every <m>\mu</m>, so <m>\Phi</m> is injective.  By
<xref ref="lem-formal-series-reduced"/>, the target is reduced; hence <m>E</m> is reduced.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
      <title>Proof of <xref ref="thm-rationally-reduced-example"/></title>
      <p>
        The uniform domain assertion is <xref ref="prop-parity-uniform-domain"/>, and nonnoetherianity is
<xref ref="prop-parity-nonnoetherian"/>.  Sheafiness is
<xref ref="thm-parity-sheafy"/>.  Reducedness of all finite iterated rational
localisations is <xref ref="thm-parity-rationally-reduced"/>.  Finally,
<xref ref="prop-weighted-chart-identification"/>, <xref ref="prop-weighted-chart-domain-nonuniform"/> identify the
rational chart <m>|W|\leq|\varpi|</m> as a nonuniform integral domain.
      </p>
    </proof>
    <remark xml:id="rem-second-example-relation">
      <title>Relation with the first construction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The finite-jet and weighted-parity examples use different mechanisms.  In the finite-jet ring,
a whole tail becomes infinitely <m>\varpi</m>-divisible and the bad chart acquires a nilpotent.  In the
weighted-parity ring, every rational calculation can be moved into a noetherian finite head and
then carried coefficientwise through a <m>c_0</m>-tail.  Its bad chart remains a domain, and
nonuniformity is witnessed by the unbounded family <m>(T_n)_{n\geq1}</m> of nonnilpotent
power-bounded elements.  The use of an infinite monomial support and of unbounded
power-bounded directions is in the tradition of the examples of Buzzard<ndash/>Verberkmoes and
Mihara; the finite-head coefficientwise descent is the additional feature that makes sheafiness
and reducedness after iterated rational localisation accessible here.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
</section>
